@page "/exgrid/data"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Data sources"
          Lead="The grid holds no data of its own. It paints a Window of rows and says when it needs others. Where the rows come from — a list, a server, a feed — and who sorts them is your application's choice.">

    <DocsSection Title="Three ways to give the grid rows">
        <MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Outlined="true" Class="docs-api-table">
            <thead><tr><th style="width: 28%">You have</th><th style="width: 30%">Use</th><th>Who sorts, filters and finds</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Every row, in memory</td><td><code>Source="GridSource.From(rows)"</code></td><td>The source, by the reference rules.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Rows on a server, a page at a time</td><td><code>Source="GridSource.Fetch(fetch)"</code></td><td>Your server, asked through <code>fetch</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Your own loading and state</td><td><code>Window</code>, <code>WindowStart</code>, <code>TotalCount</code> and <code>OnRangeNeeded</code></td><td>You, answering the grid's notifications.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </MudSimpleTable>
        <p>
            A Source is a convenience over the third form, not a different grid: it drives the same parameters, and
            the grid behaves the same either way. Pass a <code>Source</code> or a <code>Window</code>, never both —
            the grid refuses the pair rather than pick one.
        </p>
        <p>
            Two rules hold whatever you choose. <strong>A row repaints when its instance changes</strong>: replace
            rows, never rewrite them in place. And keep the source and the column list in fields, so they are the
            same objects on every render.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A server, a page at a time">
        <p>
            <code>GridSource.Fetch</code> takes a function that answers a <code>GridQuery</code> — a range of rows,
            the sort and the filter in force — with a <code>GridPage</code>: those rows and how many there are in all.
            The source fetches the first page itself, reads ahead either side of what the grid asked for
            (<code>readAheadRows</code>), cancels a request a newer one has replaced, and discards an answer that
            arrives after the question changed. Rows not yet loaded are painted as placeholders.
        </p>
        <p>
            Your server should sort and filter exactly as <code>GridSource.From</code> does; the server here simply
            runs the reference implementation, <code>GridQueryEngine.Apply</code>. To send the query over the wire,
            <code>GridViewStateJson.WriteQuery</code> writes it as JSON and <code>ReadQuery</code> reads it back on
            the server, each filter operand with its type (<a href="exgrid/columns#keeping-a-view">Keeping a
            view</a>), and the query read back is equal to the one written: two <code>GridQuery</code> values are
            equal when they ask for the same range, sort and filter, whichever lists hold them, so a cache can be
            keyed by one. Pass <code>distinctValues</code>
            to fill the filter panel's value lists and <code>find</code> to answer <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>; without
            <code>find</code>, the grid refuses to search rather than search only what it has painted. Pass
            <code>summarize</code> to answer the status-bar figures over a selection — a <code>SUM</code> on your
            server — giving what <code>GridSummary.Of</code> gives over the same rows, or declining with a reason;
            without it, no figure is shown. Subscribe to
            <code>FetchFailed</code> to show a failure yourself; unobserved, it is rethrown.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="100,000 trades from a slow server" Builtin="typeof(FetchGrid)" Mud="typeof(FetchGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/TradeServer.cs", "Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])">
            The simulated server answers after 250&nbsp;ms. Drag the scrollbar a long way: only the range you land on
            is fetched. Sort or filter, and the server answers that too.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Pushing a Window: a million rows">
        <p>
            When you hold the loading yourself, hand the grid the rows you have as <code>Window</code>, where they
            start in the result as <code>WindowStart</code>, and how many rows the result has as
            <code>TotalCount</code>. The scrollbar spans every row. When the Viewport moves past the Window, the grid
            raises <code>OnRangeNeeded</code> and paints placeholders until you answer with a new Window.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Read-ahead is yours</strong>: answer with more rows than were asked for.</li>
            <li><strong>Discard stale answers</strong>: a request that a newer one overtook should not land.</li>
            <li><strong>Set <code>IsLoading</code></strong> while an answer is on its way, or the grid shows a stale Window as if it were current.</li>
            <li><strong>Load the first page yourself.</strong> The grid never asks for rows in a result it has been told is empty.</li>
            <li><strong>Bump <code>RowSequenceVersion</code></strong> when the order of the rows changes, so a selection that would now cover other rows is dropped.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <strong>Name your rows by a Row Key</strong> when you make them afresh — fetched again, or rebuilt from a
            feed. <code>RowKey</code> is a function from a row to what tells it from every other row, such as a
            trade's id, kept in a field like the columns. A row that comes back as a new instance under the same key
            keeps its row component, and only the text that changed is written; without a key, a new instance is a
            new row. The grid refuses a Window in which a key repeats, naming the key and its rows, and to find one
            it reads every row's key in every new Window. When your Windows cannot repeat a key — cut, as here, from
            a list whose ids are unique — say so with <code>VouchesDistinctRows="true"</code>. The grid then reads
            only the keys of the rows it paints, on every render, which matters when you push a large Window at a
            time. A repeat that breaks the promise is still refused the moment it reaches the screen.
        </p>
        <p>
            The DOM does not grow with the row count — a million rows paint the same few dozen elements as a hundred.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A million rows, made on demand" Builtin="typeof(MillionRows)" Mud="typeof(MillionRowsMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/MillionTrades.cs", "Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])">
            Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>End</kbd>, or drag the scrollbar to the bottom.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Asking before the Viewport runs out">
        <p>
            However far you read ahead, the grid says it needs rows only once a row on screen is not in the Window,
            and that row stays a placeholder until your answer arrives. Over a network that is one round trip of
            placeholders each time the read-ahead runs out. <code>RangeRequestMargin</code> asks earlier: at
            <em>M</em> rows, the grid raises <code>OnRangeNeeded</code> as soon as the rows on screen and
            <em>M</em> rows either side are not all in the Window, while every row on screen may still be held.
            Scrolled steadily, the next Window lands before the Viewport reaches it.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>The request still names the rows on screen.</strong> Those are the rows the grid needs, and an
                answer that reaches them is a good answer. The margin changes only when it is asked, and the same rows
                are not asked for again until the Window moves, as without it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Read ahead at least as far as the margin.</strong> An answer of exactly the rows asked for leaves
                the margin outside the Window again, so the grid would ask at every row you scroll. That is why the
                margin is 0 unless you set it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Keep a question whose rows cover the rows on screen.</strong> While it is out, the grid asks
                again at every scroll, and each request names rows the question in flight will bring. Cancelling it in
                favour of the newer one starts the round trip again, and a held key would never see an answer. Replace a
                question only when its rows do not cover the rows on screen.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Pass <code>IsLoading</code> only while rows on screen wait.</strong> An early request finds every
                row on screen held, and the grid dims under <code>IsLoading</code>: raised for each early question, it
                would dim at every Window's edge with nothing missing. Raise it when you ask for rows that are not held,
                or when the rows on screen leave the rows held while your question is out.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            ExPivot sets its grid's margin for a report a server sends: 10 rows, against 60 rows read either side.
            <code>GridSource.Fetch</code> does not use a margin. If you set one on its grid, an early request finds the
            rows on screen in the source's Window and fetches nothing, and the source fetches when they leave it, as
            without a margin.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Asked ten rows early" Builtin="typeof(AskEarly)" Mud="typeof(AskEarlyMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/ReadAheadTrades.cs", "Examples/Grid/MillionTrades.cs", "Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])">
            The server answers after 150&nbsp;ms with 50 rows either side of the rows asked for. Click a cell and hold
            <kbd>↓</kbd> past the bottom of the grid. Without the margin, each time the rows held run out the next rows
            wait as placeholders. With it, they are asked for ten rows early, and the count of waits stays where it was.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Live updates and the Change Highlight">
        <p>
            A live feed is a stream of replaced rows: with <code>GridSource.From</code>, call
            <code>ReplaceRow(old, new)</code> for each one. Only the replaced rows render again.
        </p>
        <p>
            To show which cells just moved, answer <code>CellChangedAt</code>: asked for a row and a column, return
            when that cell's value last changed, or <code>null</code>. The grid compares nothing itself — it keeps no
            values between renders — so the answer comes from what you know, such as a feed naming the fields that
            changed. Cells that changed within <code>ChangeHighlightDuration</code> (one second by default) are marked
            in one colour, <code>--ex-change-highlight-background</code>, without animation, and the grid takes the mark
            away itself. The mark belongs to the row and column, not to the element, so it stays with its row as you scroll.
        </p>
        <p>
            Keep the delegate in a field. It is asked once per painted cell, so it should be a dictionary lookup. A
            replaced row is asked again; to change answers for rows that were not replaced, hand over a new delegate.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A live price board" Builtin="typeof(LiveQuotes)" Mud="typeof(LiveQuotesMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Quote.cs"])" />
        <p>
            For a blotter of tens of thousands of trades with ticking prices, pinned columns and Header Groups, open
            the <a href="showcase/blotter" target="_blank">trade blotter showcase</a>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

